> > >
> > > > It can be really hard to diagnose this error. Your description of the
> > > > dataset is a bit unclear. Am I correct that the y-matrix is 29 x 1872?
> > > >
> > > > It's possible that part of the problem is simply that unmarked cannot
> > > > handle that many primary periods all at once with this model, particularly
> > > > when there is a lot of missing data (which requires a bunch of additional
> > > > math).
> > > >
> > > > The design is a bit unusual relative to the way the model is most commonly
> > > > used, where primary periods represent longer time periods such as seasons
> > > > or years, and the colonization and extinction parameters make a bit more
> > > > intuitive sense. In those cases we're typically dealing with at most 10-15
> > > > primary periods. That said there shouldn't be any specific mathematical
> > > > reason why so many primary periods shouldn't work.
> > > >
> > > > I would suggest even more experiments with subsets, i.e., iteratively
> > > > increasing the number of included primary periods until you get the error,
> > > > and look at what specifically changed around that point to see if you can
> > > > identify anything unusual. I'm of course not familiar with your
> > > > objectives/design, but I would perhaps also consider: do you really need to
> > > > estimate "extinction" and "colonization" parameters here? Are you sure
> > > > dynamic occupancy is the right framework?
